# Every checkout, on every platform, byte for byte the same.
#
# This is not tidiness. index.html pins its inline import map in the
# Content-Security-Policy by SHA-256, and a hash is over bytes. Git on Windows
# checks out text with CRLF by default, which changes those bytes and so
# changes the hash — the same file, hashing differently depending on who cloned
# it.
#
# The consequence was not a failing test, it was a broken product. The Windows
# desktop build packages files from a Windows checkout, so index.html inside
# the shipped .exe would carry CRLF, the browser would compute the CRLF hash,
# compare it against the LF hash written in the policy, refuse the import map,
# and resolve no modules at all. The application would not start. It was caught
# by `npm test` failing on the Windows runner before anything was published.
#
# eol=lf forces LF in the working tree everywhere, so the bytes the hash was
# computed over are the bytes every platform gets.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries must never be touched. Listing them is what keeps `text=auto` from
# guessing wrong on a file that merely looks like text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.gif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.zip binary
*.7z binary
*.gz binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.dmg binary
*.AppImage binary
*.asar binary
*.stl binary
